<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tiye</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1398–1338 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a> · Also known as Tye, Taia, Tiy, Tiyi, The Elder Lady · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tiye was the Great Royal Wife of the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a>, mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> and grandmother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a>. Her parents, Yuya and Thuya, were non-royal and came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhmim">Akhmim</a>, where Yuya was a wealthy landowner and priest. Married to Amenhotep III by the second year of his reign, she wielded great power during his reign and her son's: in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tushratta">Tushratta</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, wrote to her directly. She outlived her husband by as many as twelve years; if she died soon after Akhenaten's 12th year (1338 BCE), she was born around 1398 BCE. In 2010 DNA analysis identified her as the Elder Lady, a mummy found in the tomb of Amenhotep II (KV35) in 1898.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiye.jpg" alt="Tiye" width="330" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Einsamer Schütze · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ägyptisches_Museum_Berlin_027_detail_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Queens such as <b>Tiye</b> and Nefertari had real influence, the God’s Wife of Amun was the highest priestess of Amun at Thebes, and Hatshepsut ruled as king.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
